#{[17592186209111 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 50 \\\\ y = {x}^{2} + 14\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is a possible value of $x$?"] [17592186209122 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 a + b = 4.5 \\\\ 3 a + 5 b = 19.5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(a, b)$. What is the value of $b$?\n\n$b =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186209123 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 u + v = 3.6 \\\\ 5 u + 3 v = 20.4\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. What is the value of $v$?\n\n$v =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186209135 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 \\\\ y = 2 ( x + 1 {)}^{2} - 5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nAt how many points do the graphs of the above equations intersect in the $xy$-plane?"] [17592186209146 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 9 \\\\ y = {x}^{2} + 2 x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?"] [17592186209147 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 49 \\\\ y = {x}^{2} - 2 x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?"] [17592186209151 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} x = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} y + {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}} = p y + {\\displaystyle \\frac{11}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.$\nIn the given system of equations, $p$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186209211 :question/problem "$3\\begin{vmatrix}2 - x & + 2\\end{vmatrix}2 - x|= 20$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $x =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186209230 :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba85a7a348b506c9a66abf9bfef664113e0b9dc947541799650abe020191d12d.png)\n\nNote: Figures not drawn to scale.\n\nTriangle $RST$ and triangle $XYZ$ are shown. The relationship between the side lengths of the two triangles is such that\n$\\frac{RS}{XY} = \\frac{ST}{YZ} = \\frac{RT}{XZ} = 4$.\nIf the measure of $∠SRT$ is $35^{∘}$, what is the measure, in degrees, of $∠YXZ$? (Disregard the degree symbol when gridding your answer.)\n\n$m∠YXZ =$\n{{field-1}} $^{∘}$"] [17592186209253 :question/problem "Consider the following system of inequalities:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}v > u + 1 \\\\ u > 8\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWhich of the following tables contains only points $(u, v)$ that satisfy this system?"] [17592186209350 :question/problem "What is the sum of the solutions to the equation given below?\n$\\mid \\frac{3}{5}x - \\frac{11}{6}|= \\frac{7}{3}$"] [17592186209351 :question/problem "$3\\begin{vmatrix}4 - a & -7\\end{vmatrix}4 - a|=-60$\nThe positive solution of the equation given above is $a =$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186209388 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 7 y = 15 \\\\ 8 x + 14 y = b\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $b$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $b$?"] [17592186209389 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 2 x + 9 y = 7 \\\\ - 6 x + 27 y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186209411 :question/problem "In the system of equations below, $m$ and $k$ are constants:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{5}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = 4 \\\\ m x - 2 y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the system has an infinite number of solutions $(x, y)$, what is the value of $m$?"] [17592186209426 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{6}} \\\\ a x + b y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{6}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $a$ and $b$ are constants. The system has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?\n\n$\\frac{a}{b} =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186209427 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{5}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{10}} \\\\ a x + b y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{10}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $a$ and $b$ are constants. The system has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?\n\n$\\frac{a}{b} =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186209878 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x,$ let's first write\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{A} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & \\cdots  & {a}_{1 n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & \\cdots  & {a}_{2 n} \\\\ \\vdots  & \\vdots  & \\ddots  & \\vdots  \\\\ {a}_{n 1} & {a}_{n 2} & \\cdots  & {a}_{n n}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & ⋯ & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1} & {a}_{n2} & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}{x}_{1}+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{x}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{21}{x}_{1}+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{2n}{x}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1}{x}_{1}+{a}_{n2}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{nn}{x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{x}_{j} \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{2j}{x}_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{nj}{x}_{j}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & \\cdots  & {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\sum _{j = 1}^{n} {a}_{1 j} {x}_{j}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\sum _{j = 1}^{n} {a}_{2 j} {x}_{j}} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\sum _{j = 1}^{n} {a}_{n j} {x}_{j}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nEach term of this product looks like $x_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j$, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f.$"] [17592186209881 :tutorial/content "Once we know how to compute the Hadamard product entry by entry, it’s helpful to understand how it behaves algebraically.\n\nIf $A, B, C \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times m}$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then the Hadamard product satisfies the following properties:\n\n- **Commutativity**\n$A \\odot B = B \\odot A$\nElement-wise multiplication doesn’t depend on order.\n- **Associativity**\n$(A \\odot B) \\odot C = A \\odot (B \\odot C)$\nWe can group Hadamard products however we like.\n- **Distributivity over Addition**\n$A \\odot (B + C) = A \\odot B + A \\odot C$\nThis works just like normal multiplication, distributing over addition, but entry by entry.\n- **Scalar Distributivity**\n$\\alpha(A \\odot B) = (\\alpha A) \\odot B = A \\odot (\\alpha B)$\nScaling before or after the Hadamard product gives the same result.\n\nThese properties make the Hadamard product easy to manipulate when working with elementwise operations.\n\nLet's look at a concrete example.\n\nGiven that\n$$\nX \\odot Y\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\nX \\odot Z^T\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nZ\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nlet's find $X \\odot (Z^T + Y \\odot Z)$.\n\nIn this case, by distributing the Hadamard product over the addition, we have\n\n$$\nX \\odot (Z^T + Y \\odot Z) = X \\odot Z^T + X \\odot (Y \\odot Z)\n$$\n\nThen, by the associativity of the Hadamard product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX⊙Z^{T} + X⊙(Y⊙Z) &= X⊙Z^{T} + (X⊙Y)⊙Z \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186212751 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1\\right)\\left(2x + \\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)(x - 7) = 0$."] [17592186212751 :question/worked-solution "We apply the zero product rule, which tells us that if the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of the expressions must be zero.\n\nSo, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 2x + \\dfrac{1}{4} = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 7 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the $1$st factor:\n$\\dfrac{3}{2}x - 1 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{3}{2}x = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = \\dfrac{2}{3}$\n- For the $2$nd factor:\n$2x + \\dfrac{1}{4} = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad 2x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -\\dfrac{1}{8}$\n- For the $3$rd factor:\n$x - 7 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 7$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are the following:\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-\\dfrac{1}{8}},\\ \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}},\\ \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}\n$$"] [17592186212752 :question/worked-solution "We apply the zero product rule, which tells us that if the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of the expressions must be zero.\n\nSo, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x + \\sqrt{6} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the $1$st factor:\n$x^2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 0$\n- For the $2$nd factor:\n$x + \\sqrt{6} = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -\\sqrt{6}$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are the following:\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt{6},\\ 0\n$$"] [17592186212761 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{64}{x + 3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{80}{x - 10}\\right)}$, where $x \\neq -3$ and $x \\neq 10$."] [17592186212762 :question/problem "Simplify\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{7}{x - 6} - \\dfrac{4}{x + 1}}$."] [17592186212763 :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{4x - 8}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{x - 2}{7}\\right)}$, where $x \\neq 2$."] [17592186212764 :question/problem "Simplify the following expression:\n$\\dfrac{5-q}{3p+4q}-\\dfrac{6p+8pq}{18p^2+24pq}$"] [17592186212771 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/609738e75d7d263268af65470237126cd34eb808e63c497fda68814f3cb82598.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 9 \\cdot 6; = 54cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 9 \\cdot 3; = 13.5cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3; = 9cm^{2}$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 6; = 9cm^{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 54 - 13.5 - 9 - 9 = 22.5 \\: \\text{cm}^2\n$$"] [17592186212772 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/034a8b7157f2b08f41d5531325ecce2205413dc02410852900046fe593e017e1.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 7; = 42$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2; = 6$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7; = 10.5$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5; = 7.5$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 42 - 6 - 10.5 - 7.5 = 18\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186212792 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$"] [17592186212799 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The mean number of likes Gina's posts received is\n$\\dfrac{8 + 16 + 32 + 14 + 20 + 6}{6} = \\dfrac{96}{6} = 16$.\nTherefore, Gina's posts received an average of $16$ likes each over the past $24$ hours.\n- Statement II is true. Since $14 < 16$, we conclude that Maria's mean number of likes per post is smaller than Gina's.\n- Statement III is true. Maria's mean is smaller than Gina's, suggesting that Gina's posts are more popular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186212800 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that Pam's data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest. Since the data set has an even number of data points ($6$), the median is the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$13, \\: 15, \\: {\\color{blue}18}, \\: {\\color{blue}20}, \\: 20, \\:21$\nThe median of the data set is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}18}+{\\color{blue}20}}{2}=\\dfrac{38}{2}=19$.\nTherefore, Pam's median daily fuel expense is $\\[math]19$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, Barbara and Pam's median daily fuel expenses are $\\[/math]25$ and $\\[math]19$, respectively.\n- Statement III is false. Since Pam's median is smaller, there is no suggestion that Pam spends more on gas per day than Barbara.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186212809 :question/worked-solution "We are asked to find the brightness range based on the formula and a recommended slider range.\n\nThe formula is\n\n$$\nB = 30x + 40\n$$\n\nThe slider should be set between $0.4$ and $0.9$. Thus, we compute:\n\n- At the minimum recommended slider level $x = 0.4$, the brightness is\n$B|= 30(0.4) + 40; = 12 + 40; = 52lumens$.\n- At the maximum recommended slider level $x = 0.9$, the brightness is\n$B|= 30(0.9) + 40; = 27 + 40; = 67lumens$.\n\nTherefore, the acceptable brightness range during use is\n\n$$\n52\\leq B\\leq 67\n$$"] [17592186212818 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must apply two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{yd}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $3$ feet per yard. We can write this as follows:\n$3\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}$\nTo determine the number of square feet in one square yard, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(3\\frac{ft}{yd})^{2}|= 3^{2}\\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}}; = 9\\frac{ft^{2}}{yd^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} & \\displaystyle  = 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\cdot  9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 33 \\cdot  9 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{297}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.95 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n4.95\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [17592186212819 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The robot's clearing rate is given as $132{,}000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 132 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  \\approx  132 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 132 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{132 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{132 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  204 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the park maintenance robot is clearing leaves at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n204\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$"] [17592186212823 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the medians for the datasets in turn.\n\n- The total number of values for store $A$ is $20$. Since our data set has an even number of data points, the median is the average of the two middle values in the positions\n$\\frac{20}{2} = 10and10 + 1 = 11$.\nNow, let’s determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the left table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.Up to the third row, the table covers $11$ positions in total.So, the $10$th and $11$th values both fall into the third row corresponding to the value $35$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$Median|= \\frac{35 + 35}{2}; = \\frac{70}{2}; = 35$.\n- The total number of values for store $B$ is $27$. Since our data set has an odd number of data points, the median is the value in position\n$\\frac{27 + 1}{2} = 14$.\nNow, let’s determine where the $14$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the right table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $8$ positions.Up to the third row, the table covers $23$ positions.So, the $14$th value falls into the third row corresponding to the value $38$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$Median|= 38$.\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the median of store A is smaller than the median of store B."] [17592186212830 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5$.\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$."] [17592186212845 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of the circle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35e2cda051f78f6ec01d4995552f893fa74eb61445645bc8f2f7b8c1858fe7eb.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{ML}{2} = \\dfrac{72}{2} = 36$\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n36 &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $12\\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$."] [17592186212854 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{12}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{t}{12}} y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $t$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t$?"] [17592186212855 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 9 + 4 y = t x \\\\ 3 y + x = - 5 x + 8\\end{array} \\right. }$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $t$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $t$?"] [17592186212855 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent distinct parallel lines. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet's start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$-9 + 4y|= tx; 4y|= tx + 9; y|= \\frac{t}{4}x + \\frac{9}{4}$.\nSo, the slope is $\\dfrac{t}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{9}{4}$.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$3y + x|=-5x + 8; 3y|=-6x + 8; y|=-2x + \\frac{8}{3}$.\nSo, the slope is $-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{8}{3}$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\ \\text{and}\\ \\dfrac{8}{3}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, equating the slopes and solving for $t$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{4} &= -2 \\\\\nt &= -2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\nt &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $-8$."] [17592186212858 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{2}} \\\\ r x - 6 y = s\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $r$ and $s$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\dfrac{r}{s}$?"] [17592186212860 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186212861 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$."] [17592186212862 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$."] [17592186212868 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 ( x - 2 ) + 3 ( 2 y + 1 ) = 39 \\\\ - 4 ( x - 2 ) + 3 ( 2 y + 1 ) = 21\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe equations above form a system, and the solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $3(2y + 1)$?"] [17592186212870 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 10 \\\\ 4 ( x + y ) + 2 y = 52\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [17592186212871 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 13 y = 80 \\\\ 13 x - 6 y = 72\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x - y$?"] [17592186212874 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = - 6 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 15\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?"] [17592186212875 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186212876 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 8 y = 32 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = - 32\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186212912 :question/problem "$\\dfrac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\dfrac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?"] [17592186212922 :question/worked-solution "Density is defined as the ratio of mass to volume:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}\n$$\n\nSince the object is a cube, its volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = s^3\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of an edge.\n\nIn this problem,\n\n- the mass is $m = 216$ grams, and\n- the edge length is $s = 6$ centimeters, so the volume is\n$V = (6)^3 = 216 \\ \\text{cm}^3$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\frac{216}{216} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the density is $1$ gram per cubic centimeter."] [17592186212972 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$60\\left(\\frac{s}{min}\\right)^{2}|= 60^{2}\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = 3, 600\\frac{s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.1 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.1 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  11.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [17592186212998 :question/problem "Calculate $x$, given that $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are complementary, and that\n$m\\angle{A} = 3x, \\qquad m\\angle{B} = 4x-8^\\circ\\,$."] [17592186213413 :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{5}(x - 7) - \\frac{3}{10}(x - 7) = 2$\nWhat value of $x$ is the solution to the given equation?"] [17592186213431 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{4}(x - 2) - \\frac{5}{6}(x - 2) =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nWhat value of $x$ is the solution to the given equation?$x =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186213457 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{x - 2} - \\frac{2}{x + 3}}$\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the above expression?"] [17592186213459 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{3x + 6}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{x + 2}{5}\\right))$, where $x \\ne - 2$?"] [17592186213460 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}$?"] [17592186213461 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{4x}{x^{2} - 7x + 12} - \\frac{x}{x - 4}$."] [17592186213468 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{p - 3pq}{5p^{2} + 10pq} + \\frac{1 - q}{p + 2q}$?"] [17592186213470 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{3x}{2x^{2} - 7x + 6} + \\frac{4x}{x - 2}$."] [17592186213472 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{36}{x + 7}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{60}{x - 2}\\right))$, where $x \\ne - 7$ and $x \\ne 2$?"] [17592186213521 :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\frac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?"] [17592186213689 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 5 y = 12 \\\\ 6 x - 10 y = b\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $b$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $b$?"] [17592186213690 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ g x + k y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $g$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\frac{g}{k}$?"] [17592186213692 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 5 \\\\ m x - 2 y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $m$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m$?"] [17592186213694 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}} \\\\ 4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186213696 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = 2 k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186213698 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 + 5 y = q x \\\\ 5 y - 3 x = 3 x + 9\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $q$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?"] [17592186213701 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $\\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}$?"] [17592186213722 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 7 y = 94 \\\\ 7 x + 3 y = 56\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x + y$?"] [17592186213723 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?"] [17592186213724 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186213725 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 5 \\\\ x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186213726 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 12 \\\\ 3 ( x + y ) + 4 y = 52\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [17592186213729 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 7 y = 9 \\\\ 7 x - 3 y = 6\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x - y$?\n\n$x - y =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186213731 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 4 = \\sqrt{2} \\\\ ( 2 x - 4 {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186213732 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 = ( x + 4 y ) ( x - 3 y ) \\\\ 7 x - 21 y = 35\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186213733 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} - {y}^{2} = 72 \\\\ 2 x + 2 y = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x - y$?"] [17592186213735 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186213739 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 12 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 42\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x + y$?"] [17592186213740 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 4 y = 36 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186213757 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{4}(x - 2) - \\frac{5}{6}(x - 2) =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nWhat value of $x$ is the solution to the given equation?$x =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186214212 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *zero product rule* states that if $ab = 0$, then $a=0$ or $b=0$. A similar rule also works for three or more factors.\n\nIn particular, if\n\n$$\na\\cdot b \\cdot c = 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\na=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad b=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c=0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if we know that the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of these expressions must be zero.\n\nLet's see how this can be used to solve polynomial equations. For example, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n(x + 12)(x - 10)(x - 11) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve it, we apply the zero product rule. So, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx + 12 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 10 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 11 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the first factor:\n$x + 12 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -12$\n- For the second factor:\n$x - 10 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 10$\n- For the third factor:\n$x - 11 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 11$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -12,\\ 10,\\ 11\n$$"] [17592186214224 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll explore how to find the area of an arbitrary triangle in the coordinate plane.\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with vertices\n\n$$\nA(2,3), \\qquad B(6,7), \\qquad C(8,2)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb6767a162efb99bf3db6e7961a15bf1c7af79a94b6ef253afdc341f2f92b27.png)\n\nFinding the area of this triangle using the usual methods is tricky because it's not easy to determine the length of the perpendicular height to a given base. So instead, we will proceed using an indirect method.\n\nFirst, we notice that we can **inscribe** our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b57fe9e05e0afa8228a9b5f0cbb65a14109660457880a4f461d0689e7593569.png)\n\nInscribing a triangle in a rectangle means drawing a rectangle around the triangle in such a way that the triangle lies completely inside the rectangle and each of the triangles vertices touches the rectangle.\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area that we denote by $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area that we denote by $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area that we denote by $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nIn other words, the area of the required (blue) triangle equals the area of the orange rectangle minus the areas of the three triangles with areas $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$. Notice that it's much easier to find the areas of these three triangles compared to the blue triangle, since their bases and heights are easily determined from the diagram.\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 5; = 30$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4; = 8$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5; = 5$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186214229 :tutorial/content "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214249 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(1, 000\\frac{m}{km})^{2}|= 1, 000^{2}\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}; = 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [17592186214272 :tutorial/content "In the work that follows, it will be useful to remember the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^n b^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214315 :tutorial/content "When rewriting an exponential model in different time units, a common mistake is mixing up whether to *multiply* or *divide* by the conversion factor.\n\n- When going from longer units to shorter units, we *divide*. For example, suppose\n$B(t) = 1{,}200 \\cdot (1.10)^t$\nmodels a bacterial culture $t$ days after it is placed in a dish. Let's assume ee want to express this in terms of hours instead of days. Since there are $24$ hours in a day, we substitute\n$t = \\dfrac{h}{24}$.\nWe *divide* by $24$ so that $h=24$ hours corresponds to $t=1$ day.\n$b(h) = B\\!\\left(\\dfrac{h}{24}\\right) = 1{,}200 \\cdot (1.10)^{h/24}$.\n- When going from shorter units to longer units, we *multiply*. For example, suppose\n$V(t) = 5{,}000 \\cdot (1.03)^t$\nmodels the number of video views $t$ seconds after upload. Let's assume we want to express this in terms of minutes instead of seconds. Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, we substitute\n$t = 60m$.\nWe *multiply* by $60$ so that $m=1$ minute corresponds to $t=60$ seconds.\n$v(m) = V(60m) = 5{,}000 \\cdot (1.03)^{60m}$."] [17592186216768 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$\\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186216809 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186216813 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?"] [17592186216813 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [17592186216814 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186216816 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186216817 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?"] [17592186216819 :question/worked-solution "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4, -8)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2} - 8x + 16) - 8 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} - 8ax + 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= -8a \\\\\nc &= 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + (-8a) + (16a - 8) \\\\\n&= 9a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at two points and has its vertex at $(4, -8)$ below the $x$-axis, it must open upward, meaning $a > 0$.\n\nNow, let's go through all the options and check which ones give $a > 0{:}$\n\n- Option I, $-8{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-8; 9a|=-8 + 8; 9a|= 0; a|= 0≯0\\cdot$\n- Option II, $-1{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-1; 9a|=-1 + 8; 9a|= 7; a|= \\frac{7}{9} > 0✓$\n- Option III, $10{:}$\n$9a - 8|= 10; 9a|= 10 + 8; 9a|= 18; a|= 2 > 0✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186216822 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186216826 :question/worked-solution "We're given the equation of circle $\\text{C}$\n\n$$\n(x+3)^2+(y-2)^2=25\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate it by $4$ units right and double its radius.\n\nFor circle $\\text{C}{:}$\n\n- For each point on the circle, we must add $4$ to each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on circle $\\text{D}$. So, the new $x$-coordinates of the points on circle $\\text{D}$ are given by\n$x_{\\textrm{new}} = x + 4$.\nThis equation is equivalent to\n$x = x_{\\textrm{new}} - 4$.\n- We also must double the radius of circle $\\text{C}$ to get the corresponding radius of circle $\\text{D}$. The right-hand side of the equation of circle $\\text{C}$ represents the square of its radius:\n$r^2 = 25$\nDoubling the radius of circle $\\text{C}$, the new right-hand side of the equation of circle $\\text{D}$ must be\n$r_{new}|= 2r; r_{new}^{2}|= (2r)^{2}; = 4r^{2}; = 4 \\cdot 25; = 100$.\n\nTo get the equation of circle $\\text{D}$, we substitute into the equation of circle $\\text{C}$ and update the right-hand side accordingly to give the new squared radius. Doing so, we get\n\n$$\n((x_{new} - 4) + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} = 100\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\n\\big((x - 4)+3\\big)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the expression, we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of circle $\\text{D}$ is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$"] [17592186216833 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we have $g(0) = -1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $b{:}$\n$g(0)|=-1; a^{0} + b|=-1; 1 + b|=-1; b|=-2$\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(3,25)$, we have $g(3) = 25$. Then, using the fact that $b=-2$, we can determine the value of $c$, as follows:\n$g(3)|= 25; a^{3} + (-2)|= 25; a^{3} - 2|= 25; a^{3}|= 27; a|= \\sqrt[3]{27}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 + (-2) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216834 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection."] [17592186216835 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [17592186216841 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186216842 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216843 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d{:}$\n$\\dfrac{20}{a} = \\dfrac{20}{b} - \\dfrac{20}{c} - \\dfrac{20}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [17592186216848 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, with its maximum at $x=0$, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, and has no maximum.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$,\nthe base is $1.2>1$. So, the function has no maximum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n- For the function $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$g(0)|= 27 \\cdot (0.75)^{0}; = 27$.\nThus, the maximum value is $27$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the maximum as a coefficient.\n- For the function $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$h(0)|= 9 \\cdot (0.75)^{0} + 4; = 9 + 4; = 13$.\nThus, the maximum value is $13$. However, neither the constant term ($4$) nor the coefficient ($9$) equals $13$. Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186216852 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 4 x - y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{y}{ {x}^{2}}} = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186216853 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 2 \\\\ 3 x - y = 14\\end{array} \\right. }$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186216859 :question/worked-solution "Notice that each table gives function values at $x=-2,0$, and $2$.\n\nTo determine which table is correct, we first express $y$ in terms of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 12 \\\\\n2y &= 12 - 4x \\\\\ny &= 6 - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of the given values of $x$ in turn.\n\n- For $x = -2{:}$\n$y|= 6 - 2(-2); = 6 + 4; = 10$\n- For $x = 0{:}$\n$y|= 6 - 2(0); = 6 - 0; = 6$\n- For $x = 2{:}$\n$y|= 6 - 2(2); = 6 - 4; = 2$\n\nTherefore, the filled-in table is the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $10$ |\n| $0$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |"] [17592186216860 :question/problem "$S = 2 \\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot x + \\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot 2 + x \\cdot 2 \\right)$\nThe length of a container's base is a quarter of its width $x$, and the height of the container is $2$ feet. The function $S$, given above, models the surface area of the container as a rectangular solid, expressed in square feet. What expression represents the area of the container's base, in square feet?"] [17592186216874 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + y = 6 \\\\ 9 x + 3 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, -3t+6)$\n2. $(3t-6,t)$\n3. $(2t, -6t+6)$"] [17592186216874 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3t + 6$\nHence, $(t,-3t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $x$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; 3x|=-y + 6; x|= \\frac{1}{3}(-y + 6); x|=-\\frac{1}{3}y + 6; x|=-\\frac{1}{3}t + 2$\nThis gives $\\left(-\\dfrac13t + 2, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-6,t)$. Hence, $(3t-6,t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(2t, h(t))$. So, we set $x = 2t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3(2t) + 6; y|=-6t + 6$\nHence, $(2t,-6t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186216884 :question/worked-solution "First, since\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{x+1}\n$$\n\nwe can write $f$ in terms of $g$ by multiplying both sides by $(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle g ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( x )}{x + 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) \\cdot  g ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = ( x + 1 ) \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( x )}{x + 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) \\cdot  g ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = \\cancel{( x + 1 )} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{f ( x )}{\\cancel{x + 1}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) g ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = f ( x )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)g(x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting in each given value of $x$, we find coordinates on the line $y=f(x){:}$\n\n- For $x=2$, we have\n$f(2)=(2+1)\\cdot g(2)=(3)\\cdot 1=3$.\nSo, the point $(2,3)$ is on the line.\n- For $x=4$, we have\n$f(4)=(4+1)\\cdot g(4)=(5)\\cdot 2=10$.\nSo, the point $(4,10)$ is on the line.\n- For $x=8$, we have\n$f(8)=(8+1)\\cdot g(8)=(9)\\cdot \\dfrac{8}{3}=24$.\nSo, the point $(8,24)$ is on the line.\n\nSo, we now have a table of values for $y = f(x)$.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ |\n| $8$ | $24$ |\n\nSince $f$ is a linear function, it can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=mx+c\n$$\n\nNow, we find the slope $m$ using the points\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1)=(2,3)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2)=(4,10){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 - 3}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept $c$ using the point $(2,3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= \\frac{7}{2}(2) + c \\\\\n3 &= 7 + c \\\\\nc &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2}x - 4\n$$"] [17592186216893 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $12$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186216895 :question/problem "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is one-and-a-half times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find an expression for the surface area of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186216909 :question/problem "Triangles $PQR$ and $XYZ$ are such that\n$PQ=5,\\quad QR = 7, \\quad\\text{and}\\quad XY=15,\\quad YZ=21$.\nWhich of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. The lengths of the sides $\\overline{PR}$ and $\\overline{XZ}$.\n2. $m\\angle{P} = m\\angle{X}$.\n3. The length of the side $\\overline{XZ}$.\n4. No additional information is needed."] [17592186216929 :question/worked-solution "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/e3/e3332679e820e6a3e84e4d808a8c3eb25905336caaf5b3b05f78e029ee503bc7.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 2:1$\n- The ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} =\\sqrt{3}:1$\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 2:\\sqrt{3}$\n\nThese ratios are true for any $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. But the converse is also true: if at least one of these ratios is true in a right triangle, then this is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our right triangles in turn.\n\n- Triangle I is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg is the following:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 12\\sqrt{2}:12 = \\sqrt{2}:1 \\neq 2:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Triangle II is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg is the following:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} = 16:9 \\neq \\sqrt{3}:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Triangle III is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Indeed, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg is the following:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 10:5\\sqrt{3} = 2:\\sqrt{3} \\quad {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the triangle $\\textrm{III}$ is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle."] [17592186216952 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its **minimum** at $x=1$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$,\nthe base is $0.7\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 1$ and has **no** minimum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function\n$g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$,\nthe base is $1.4>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$g(1)|= 12 \\cdot (1.4)^{1} - 3; = 12 \\cdot 1.4 - 3; = 16.8 - 3; = 13.8$.\nThe minimum value is $13.8$, which is **not** equal to the coefficient ($12$) or the constant term $(-3)$. Hence, the equation of $g$ does *not* display the minimum.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$,\nthe base is $1.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$h(1)|= 15 \\cdot (1.5)^{1} - 7.5; = 15 \\cdot 1.5 - 7.5; = 22.5 - 7.5; = 15$.\nThus, the minimum value is $15$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $h$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186216955 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can take out a factor of $-1$ from the factor $(2-x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{8}(2 - x)(x + 1)(x + 5) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(x - 2)(x + 1)(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-5,-1,2$.\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{8}(0 - 2)(0 + 1)(0 + 5); =-\\frac{1}{8} \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (1) \\cdot (5); = \\frac{5}{4}$.\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b929a17573109c8d063ec1d66ee5eda278bab6895530e08fad807ede7c363de8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq a \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186216956 :question/worked-solution "First, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,4$, where $x=4$ is a \"double root.\"\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|=-5(0 + 1)(0 - 4)^{2}; =-5 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (-4)^{2}; =-80$.\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc1351a5714f0b77c4509ef83efe205c45c2dc529f10d6831b1ad3a72f58f97c.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 4\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only $x=1$ (option III) does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186216957 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+2x+5$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 2^2-4(1)(5) = -16 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=2$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= (0 - 2)(0^{2} + 2(0) + 5); = (-2) \\cdot (5); =-10$.\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c7ef60b2b4e65b4f62c30bedd785608bbc3dd2daad8463c8d979897549c413e.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n0 \\leq a \\leq 2\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186216983 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 4, 4, 4, 4, 6 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 12, 12, 12 \\\\ C: & 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from smallest to largest."] [17592186216984 :question/problem "A courier company recorded delivery times, in minutes, for two routes. The times for individual deliveries are given below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Route 1: & 18, 22, 27, 35, 44, 50, 63, 70 \\\\ Route 2: & 38, 39, 39, 40, 40, 41, 41, 42\\end{bmatrix}$\nCompare the mean absolute deviations of both groups."] [17592186216992 :question/worked-solution "We are told that\n\n$$\nd(x) = \\dfrac{r(x)}{x + 1}\n$$\n\nand that $r$ is a linear function. To isolate $r(x)$, we multiply both sides of the equation by $(x + 1){:}$\n\n$$\nr(x) = d(x) \\cdot (x + 1)\n$$\n\nUsing the values from the table, we compute $r(x)$ at three $x$-values.\n\n- When $x = -3$, we have\n$r(-3)|= d(-3) \\cdot (-3 + 1); = (-6) \\cdot (-2); = 12$.\n- When $x = 1$, we have\n$r(1)|= d(1) \\cdot (1 + 1); = 2 \\cdot 2; = 4$.\n- When $x = 3$, we have\n$r(3)|= d(3) \\cdot (3 + 1); = 0 \\cdot 4; = 0$.\n\nNow, we have three points on the graph of $y=r(x)$, namely, $(-3, 12)$, $(1, 4)$, and $(3, 0)$.\n\nSince $r$ is linear, we want to find the equation representing the line that passes through these points in the form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nWe use the two given points, say\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1) = (-3, 12)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2, y_2) = (1, 4)\n$$\n\nto find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (4 - 12)/(1 - (-3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{4} \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute any point the line passes through, such as $(3, 0)$, into the equation to solve for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2x + b \\\\\n0 &= -2(3) + b \\\\\nb &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line $y=r(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 6\n$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept is the value of $r(0)$, which we evaluate by substituting $x=0$ into the obtained equation:\n\n$$\nr(0) = -2(0) + 6 = 6\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept of the graph of $y = r(x)$ is $(0, 6)$."] [17592186216993 :question/worked-solution "When a fixed constant is added to every data value, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $29$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $15$th value) of $3.4$. Adding $0.3$ to every value shifts the median to $3.7$. So, the median increases by $0.3$.\n- The mean depends on every value, so adding the same number to all values will increase the mean by the same number. If we denote the $29$ different weights by $w_1, w_2, \\ldots, w_{29}$ and add $0.3$ to each, then the new mean is:\n$((w_{1} + 0.3) + (w_{2} + 0.3) + ⋯ + (w_{29} + 0.3))/(29)|= (0.3 \\cdot 29 + (w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}))/(29); = 0.3 + \\frac{w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}}{29}; = 0.3 + 3.4; = 3.7$\nSo, the mean also increases by $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186216996 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$x|= 3w - 7; x + 7|= 3w; \\frac{x + 7}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7)$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216997 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$x|= 3w + 5; x - 5|= 3w; \\frac{x - 5}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5)$.\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216998 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$x|= \\frac{1}{3}h - 4; x + 4|= \\frac{1}{3}h; 3(x + 4)|= h; h|= 3(x + 4)$.\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186217412 :question/problem "Two local running clubs tracked the distances, in kilometers, run by their members during a weekly fitness challenge. The distances run by individual club members are given below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Club A: & 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 9, 9 \\\\ Club B: & 5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Club A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the standard deviation of Club B."] [17592186217413 :question/problem "Two different stores recorded the number of customers arriving at their stores each hour. The data for both stores is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Store A: & 15, 15, 16, 16, 17, 17, 17, 17 \\\\ Store B: & 5, 5, 6, 15, 25, 26, 26, 30\\end{bmatrix}$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Store A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the MAD of Store B."] [17592186217414 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 7, 7, 7, 7, 8 \\\\ B: & 10, 10, 10, 10, 10 \\\\ C: & 1, 2, 4, 5, 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from **smallest to largest**."] [17592186217415 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 10, 20, 24, 25, 26 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 13, 13, 13 \\\\ C: & 11, 11, 11, 11, 11\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their mean absolute deviation (MAD), from **smallest to largest**."] [17592186217468 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 20 x + 13 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186217472 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection.\n\nThe point of intersection is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [17592186217482 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 5 {x}^{2} - 15 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186217483 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}$\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?"] [17592186217484 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$\\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [17592186217492 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 12 x + 20 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186217509 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x + 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186217515 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {x}^{2} - 6 x + 10 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186217518 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 4 x + 3 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186217525 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?"] [17592186217538 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$"] [17592186217541 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 8 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186217565 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x - 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186217567 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 1 \\\\ - x + 3 y = 2 \\\\ x + y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186217583 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$"] [17592186217606 :question/problem "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is two times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Which of the following gives the surface area of the combined solid?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186217608 :question/problem "Two identical cylinders have a height of $10$ centimeters and a base radius of $5$ centimeters. The cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find the surface area, in square centimeters, of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*\n\nThe surface area of the combined solid is\n{{field-1}}\n$cm^{2}$."] [17592186217610 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $15$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186217749 :question/problem "Triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$ are such that\n$AB = 9, AC = 10, andDE = 18, DF = 20$.\nWhich of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. $m∠C = m∠F$.\n2. The lengths of the sides $BC^(―)$ and $EF^(―)$.\n3. $m∠B = m∠E$.\n4. No additional information is needed."] [17592186217751 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 1 \\\\ 2 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ 3 x + 6 y = 3\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [17592186217762 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} = 2xy - x$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$."] [17592186217793 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$x^{2}ydx - (x^{3} + 2y^{4})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{4}{y}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x, y) = x^{2}y, Q(x, y) =-x^{3} - 2y^{4}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(y) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."] [17592186217796 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$(7x - 4xy)dx + dy = 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}} where $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$.Let's denote\n$P(x, y) = - (4xy - 7x), Q(x, y) = 1$.Now, we compute the partial derivatives:$-4x$ {{field-3}} $1$ {{field-4}}"] [17592186217797 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} =-x(y^{2} + 1)$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\inth(y)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$."] [17592186218242 :tutorial/content "Finally, we'll discuss how to bound the *difference* between two statistics. This is often useful when comparing two data sets, and we want to see how large or small differences in their metrics could be.\n\n![](images/89/898b8ceb1d145a843b51ff173c5471675b403189781793fc6156222c844d4c23.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. What is the *largest possible difference* between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$? We can write this symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B) - \\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the *largest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *largest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *smallest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the largest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our data set as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the *largest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.\nValue $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{max}}$$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$$80-84$$2$$84$$2\\cdot 84 = 168$$84-88$$3$$88$$3\\cdot 88 = 264$$88-92$$7$$92$$7\\cdot 92 = 644$$92-96$$5$$96$$5\\cdot 96 = 480$$96-100$$3$$100$$3\\cdot 100 = 300$\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n$max(mean(B))|= \\frac{168 + 264 + 644 + 480 + 300}{2 + 3 + 7 + 5 + 3}; = \\frac{1856}{20}; = 92.8$.\n- Next, we compute the smallest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $36 - 39$. Therefore, the *smallest* possible value of the median is\n$\\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 36$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the largest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(mean(B)) - min(median(A)) &= 92.8 - 36 \\\\\n&= 56.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186218245 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write exponential functions in many different, equivalent forms. Depending on the situation, we may prefer one form over another if it displays characteristics we're interested in more clearly.\n\nFor example, consider the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=35\\cdot(1.4)^{x+1}\n$$\n\nIt's possible to express this function in a variety of different forms using the laws of exponents. Let's go through some of the most common:\n\n- We can write the function $f$ in an equivalent form with $x$ only in the exponent using the product rule in reverse.\n$f(x)|= 35 \\cdot (1.4)^{x + 1}; = 35 \\cdot (1.4)^{x} \\cdot (1.4)^{1}; = 35 \\cdot (1.4)^{x} \\cdot 1.4; = 35 \\cdot 1.4 \\cdot (1.4)^{x}; = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^{x}$\n- Similarly, we can write the function $f$ in an equivalent form with $(x-1)$ in the exponent as follows:\n$f(x)|= 49 \\cdot (1.4)^{x}; = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^{x - 1 + 1}; = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^{x - 1} \\cdot (1.4)^{1}; = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^{x - 1} \\cdot 1.4; = 49 \\cdot 1.4 \\cdot (1.4)^{x - 1}; = 68.6 \\cdot (1.4)^{x - 1}$\n\nTherefore, our function $f$ has the following equivalent forms:\n\n- $f(x) = 35\\cdot(1.4)^{x+1}$\n- $f(x) = 49 \\cdot (1.4)^x$\n- $f(x) = 68.6 \\cdot (1.4)^{x-1}$\n\nPlease note that exponential functions have *many* equivalent forms. For the function $f$ here, this is only three of them."] [17592186218260 :tutorial/content "We now consider a slightly more involved function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot m^x + n\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Suppose we are told that the graph of $y = f(x)$ in the $xy$-plane passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(0, 1\\right)\n$$\n\nand the product of $m$ and $n$ is $-12$.\n\nLet's use this information to find the values of $m$ and $n$. To do this, we examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, 1\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = 1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $n{:}$\n$f(0)|= 1; 2 \\cdot m^{0} + n|= 1; 2 \\cdot 1 + n|= 1; 2 + n|= 1; 2 + n - 2|= 1 - 2; n|=-1$\n- Since $mn=-12$ and using the fact that $n = -1$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $m{:}$\n$mn|=-12; m(-1)|=-12; - m|=-12; (-m)(-1)|= (-12)(-1); m|= 12$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $m=12$ and $n=-1$."] [17592186218318 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to determine which values of a polynomial $p(x)$ are possible over a specified $x$-interval without needing to compute exact outputs or plot the full graph.\n\nFor example, in the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny=2(x+1)(x-1)(x-3)\n$$\n\ncontains the point $(a,b)$, where\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWhat can we say about the possible values of $b$ on this interval?\n\nFirst, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,1,3$.\n- Since the leading coefficient is positive, we have:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y → ∞ & as & x → ∞ \\\\ y → - ∞ & as & x → - ∞\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= 2(0 + 1)(0 - 1)(0 - 3); = 2 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-3); = 6$.\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c56df4301167d81ecc6eb3caaf58f3e5048033eb0b6eaf14110249ccd8d69d8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThis interval is highlighted on the graph.\n\nNotice that on this interval, the graph of the cubic lies on or above the $x$-axis (it meets the axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ and is positive in between). So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b$ can't be negative."] [17592186218326 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our original dataset:\n$4, \\quad 6, \\quad 7, \\quad 8, \\quad 9$\n\nRecall that:\n\n- The mean is $6.8$.\n- The median is $7$.\n- The range is $5$.\n\nNow, suppose that we \"push away from the median\" by adding $2$ to every value above the median and subtracting $2$ from every value below the median, so the new dataset is\n${\\color{blue}2}, \\; {\\color{blue}4}, \\; 7, \\; {\\color{blue}10}, \\; {\\color{blue}11}$.\n\nLet's discuss how this transformation affects the statistics we found earlier:\n\n- The *mean* remains the same! This is because the change in the sum is balanced on either side of the median. So, if we compute the net change between the old dataset and the new one, we have\n$Change in sum|= (2 - 4) + (4 - 6) + (7 - 7) + (10 - 8) + (11 - 9); = (-2) + (-2) + 0 + 2 + 2; = 0$.\nComputing the new mean explicitly, we have\n$\\dfrac{2+4+7+10+11}{5} = 6.8$.\n- The *median* also remains the same. Each value is pushed away from the median ($7$), so their order relative to it does change, and neither does the middle value.\n- The *range* increases because the new data set is formed by pushing away from the median, increasing the dataset's spread. Note that the new range is\n$11 - 2 = 9$.\n- The *standard deviation* increases since the data are more spread out.\n\nLet's see an example where we multiply each member of a data set by a fixed number."] [17592186218328 :tutorial/content "> The volume flux of a fluid, in cubic millimeters per second, through a surface perpendicular to its motion is defined as the product of the area of the surface, in square millimeters, and the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nSo, we've seen how the above sentence can be translated into the following mathematical formula:\n\n$$\nF = A \\cdot v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the volume flux, in cubic millimeters per second,\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square millimeters, and\n- $v$ is the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of the formula. The formula *cannot* be correct if the units on both sides are not the same!\n\n- We're told that volume flux $F$ has units of cubic millimeters per second, or\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n- On the right-hand side, we have a product of area, in square millimeters, and velocity, in millimeters per second. Therefore, the units of $A\\cdot v$ are\n$\\textrm{mm}^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n\nTherefore, the units on both sides of our formula are the same:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{\\textrm{units of } F} \n= \n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{ {\\textrm{units of } {A}} \\,\\cdot\\, v}\n$$\n\nSince the units on both sides are the same, our formula\n\n$$\nF = A\\cdot v\n$$\n\nappears to be correct.\n\n**Watch out!** If the units are the same on both sides of a certain formula, this *does not guarantee* that the formula is correct!\n\nThe best way to guarantee that a formula is correct is by reading the provided definition carefully, writing down a formula from the definition, and then double-checking your answer by comparing the units. Remember, if the units do not balance, then the formula *cannot* be correct!"] [17592186218342 :tutorial/content "Consider the following functions, each defined on\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\n- $f(x) = 2\\cdot(0.5)^x$\n- $g(x) = 4\\cdot(2.5)^x$\n- $h(x) = 4\\cdot(2.5)^x+1$\n\nOn the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a> 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its minimum at $x=0$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=2\\cdot(0.5)^x$,\nthe base is $0.5\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 0$ and has *no minimum*. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function $g(x)=4\\cdot(2.5)^x$, the base is $2.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$g(0)|= 4 \\cdot (2.5)^{0}; = 4$.\nThus, the minimum value is $4$, which is exactly the coefficient shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=4\\cdot(2.5)^x+1$,\nthe base is $2.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$h(0)|= 4 \\cdot (2.5)^{0} + 1; = 4 + 1; = 5$.\nThe minimum value is $5$, which is *not* equal to the coefficient ($4$) or the constant term ($1$). Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the minimum as a constant term or a coefficient."] [17592186218357 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have the functions\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nSo, the partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\neq\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},$ the equation is *not* exact.\n\nWe now consider the integrating factor\n\n$$\nI(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the original equation by $I(x,y),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}\\Big[y\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y\\Big]=0,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\ne^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}x + xe^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nLet's define the new functions $M$ and $N$ as\n\n$$\nM(x,y)=e^{y},\\qquad N(x,y)=xe^{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=e^{y},\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x}=e^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x},$ the transformed equation is exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the original equation."] [17592186220968 :question/worked-solution "We compare the volumes of two cylinders using their formulas, given that the second pipe’s volume is $162$ times greater than the first. Recall that the volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet the volume of the smaller pipe be $V_1{:}$\n\n$$\nV_1 = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told the second pipe has $162$ times the volume of the first pipe, so its volume, $V_2$, is\n\n$$\nV_2 = 162 \\cdot \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSuppose the second pipe has radius $R$ and height $H$. Its volume is\n\n$$\nV_2 = \\pi R^2 H\n$$\n\nNow, we set the two expressions for $V_2$ equal and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπR^{2}H &= 162 \\cdot πr^{2}h \\\\\nR^{2}H &= 162r^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we test the choices to find which pair of values for $R$ and $H$ makes this equation true.\n\n- For $R = 36r$ and $H = \\dfrac{1}{2}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (36r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 1296r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 4h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 144r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 12r$ and $H = \\dfrac{9}{5}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (12r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 144r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 259.2r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 18h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n\n- For $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (9r)^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 81r^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 162r^{2}h✓$\nThis matches the given equation.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h$."] [17592186220975 :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nWe are given the equation\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y + 4)^2 = 16\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n(x - 7)^2 + (y - (-4))^2 = 4^2\n$$\n\nComparing the last equation with the general equation of a circle, we obtain that the center of our circle has coordinates $(7,-4)$, and its radius is $4$.\n\nNow, for a point to be inside a circle, the distance from that point to the center of the circle must be less than the radius.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to find which of the given points is such that its distance to $(7,-4)$ is less than $4$ (the radius of the circle).\n\n- For $(7,-4)$, this is the center of the circle, and therefore must lie in its interior.\n- For $(12,-4)$, we get\n$\\sqrt{(12 - 7)^{2} + (-4 - (-4))^{2}}|= \\sqrt{5^{2} + 0}; = \\sqrt{25}; = 5; ≮4$.\n- For $(9,-4)$, we obtain\n$\\sqrt{(9 - 7)^{2} + (-4 - (-4))^{2}}|= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0}; = \\sqrt{4}; = 2; < 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186221006 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = 12 \\\\ 4 ( x + 3 y ) + y = 55\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [17592186221007 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186221008 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y$?"] [17592186221009 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $2x + 3y$?"] [17592186221010 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 p - 7 q = 2 \\\\ - 3 p + 4 q = 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(p, q)$. What is the value of $pq$?"] [17592186221014 :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $y$ be the volume (in milliliters) of the first and second solutions, respectively.\n\nLet's establish the linear equations that model the given situation.\n\n- The total volume was $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$, so\n$x + y = 90.0$.\n- We are given that the total salt content is $25.0\\%$ of $90.0\\,\\text{mL}$. Therefore,\n$25.0% \\cdot 90.0mL|= 0.25 \\cdot 90.0mL; = 22.50mL$.\nWe are also told that the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt and the second was $40.0\\%$ salt. Therefore, we get the equation\n$0.10x + 0.40y = 22.50$.\n\nPutting the equations together, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 90.0 \\\\ 0.10 x + 0.40 y = 22.50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using the substitution method. We solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 90.0 \\\\\ny &= 90.0 - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute\n\n$$\ny = 90.0 - x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x + 0.40y &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 0.40(90.0 - x) &= 22.50 \\\\\n0.10x + 36.0 - 0.40x &= 22.50 \\\\\n- 0.30x &= -13.50 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-13.50}{-0.30} \\\\\nx &= 45.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the first solution was $10.0\\%$ salt by volume, the volume of salt in the first solution used was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.10x &= 0.10(45.0) \\\\\n&= 4.5mL\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221018 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f9f13b5becabd1cdc61e4409ab8301fc73f1dbf25e044afc1a9246691e88a3a.png)\n\nWhat system of linear equations represents the graph shown above?\n\n${-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0$\n\n$\\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{III. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 5 \\\\ 2 x + y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = - 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$"] [17592186221018 :question/worked-solution "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y + 5|= 2(x - 0); y|= 2x - 5$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|= 2x - 5; - 2x + y|=-5$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x; 2y|=-x; x + 2y|= 0$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\n{-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**"] [17592186221019 :question/problem "![](images/be/be55795a3af8da6c91c4a21b813a438622f49801992bbd9855597bb7e56b88b2.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of equations has the same solution as the system of equations graphed above?\n\n$\\textrm{I. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 4 \\\\ y = 1\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n${x = 1; y =-4$\n\n$\\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 1 \\\\ y = 4\\end{array} \\right. }$"] [17592186221020 :question/problem "![](images/57/5747249bf065187e832b2ebef180ddfa91e4c4c0adb9ca11907ae9d26e8cf903.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represents the following system of linear equations?\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = - 3 \\\\ 2 x - 5 y = 10\\end{array} \\right. }$"] [17592186221020 :question/worked-solution "To determine which graph represents this system, we first rewrite each equation in slope-intercept form.\n\n- For the first equation, we have the following:\n$x + 3y|=-3; 3y|=-x - 3; y|=-\\frac{1}{3}x - 1$\nThis line has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-1$.\n- For the second equation, we have the following:\n$2x - 5y|= 10; - 5y|=-2x + 10; y|= \\frac{2}{5}x - 2$\nThis line has a slope of $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ and a $y$-intercept of $-2$.\n\nNow, we draw both lines on the same graph.\n\n- For the first line, $y = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x - 1{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -1$.If $y = 0$, then $x = -3$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -1)$ and $(-3, 0)$.\n- For the second line, $y = \\dfrac{2}{5}x - 2{:}$If $x = 0$, then $y = -2$.If $y = 0$, then $x = 5$.So, the line passes through points $(0, -2)$ and $(5, 0)$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following graph, where the intersection of the lines represents the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/2c/2ca4fd9da55dfbe61ead9f6b933d2a85a5a72cacbd59fadb9b6c1fafbb84bbdd.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is graph II."] [17592186221026 :question/worked-solution "In $\\triangle ABC$, the sum of the measures of the interior angles is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle A + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, it must have at least two equal angles. Let's consider the distinct possibilities.\n\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then $70^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle B = m\\angle C$, then we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠B + m∠B|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + 2m∠B|= 180^{∘}; 70^{∘} + 2m∠B|= 180^{∘}; 2m∠B|= 110^{∘}; m∠B|= 55^{∘}$.\nSo, $55^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n- If $m\\angle C = m\\angle A = 70^\\circ$, then we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; 70^{∘} + m∠B + 70^{∘}|= 180^{∘}; 140^{∘} + m∠B|= 180^{∘}; m∠B|= 40^{∘}$.\nSo, $40^\\circ$ is a possible measure of $\\angle B$.\n\nTherefore, among the choices, only $62^\\circ$ is not a possible measure of $\\angle B$."] [17592186221036 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx + 2ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{m}{2n}x + \\frac{1}{2n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}$.\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx - ny = 1 & ⇒y = \\frac{m}{n}x - \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x - 2y = 1 & ⇒y = 4x - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx + ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{2m}{n}x + \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186221041 :question/worked-solution "To find Leo’s total earnings $t$, we use the earnings per box and apply the coordinates from the graph to evaluate $t$ using the linear equation that models the situation.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of boxes of pencils and $y$ be the number of boxes of pens.\n\nThus, the equation that translates this situation is\n\n$$\n7x + 14y = t\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see:\n\n- The line crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,10)$, this means if he sold $10$ boxes of pens and $0$ of pencils, he earned\n$t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(0) + 14(10); = 140$.\n- The line crosses the $x$-axis at $(20,0)$, this means if he sold $20$ boxes of pencils and $0$ of pens, he earned\n$t|= 7x + 14y; = 7(20) + 14(0); = 140$.\n\nTherefore, in either case, Leo would have earned a total of\n\n$$\nt = \\[math]140\n$$"] [17592186221042 :question/problem "The functions $u$ and $v$ are defined as\n$u(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5}x - 8 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v(x) = \\dfrac{2}{5}x + 6$.\nIf the function $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?"] [17592186221064 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $q$ is a constant.\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 x + 10 y = - 25 \\\\ y - 8 q y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{5}} + 6 x\\end{array} \\right. }$\nGiven that the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?"] [17592186221064 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- First equation:\n$15x + 10y|=-25; 10y|=-15x - 25; y|= \\frac{-15}{10}x + \\frac{-25}{10}; y|=-\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{5}{2}$\n- Second equation:\n$y - 8qy|= \\frac{7}{5} + 6x; (1 - 8q)y|= 6x + \\frac{7}{5}; y|= (6)/((1 - 8q))x + (7)/(5(1 - 8q))$\n\nTherefore, for this equation to have the same slope as the first equation, we match the slopes and solve for $q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6)/((1 - 8q)) &= -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= -3(1 - 8q) \\\\\n12 &= -3 + 24q \\\\\n24q &= 15 \\\\\nq &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the system has no solution for\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$"] [17592186221065 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right. }$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?"] [17592186221065 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\nm = kw + b\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept:\n\n- First equation:\n$7 - 9m|= aw; - 9m|= aw - 7; m|=-\\frac{a}{9}w + \\frac{7}{9}$\n- Second equation:\n$-9m + 6w|=-\\frac{3}{2}; - 9m|=-6w - \\frac{3}{2}; m|= \\frac{2}{3}w + \\frac{1}{6}$\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{9}\\right\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, we match the slopes and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{a}{9} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n- a &= 6 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221066 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - 5 \\\\ y = d x + 9\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $d$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $d$?"] [17592186221070 :question/worked-solution "We begin by examining the triangle formed by lines $p$, $q$, and $m$. This triangle has three interior angles:\n\n- One angle is formed at the intersection of lines $p$ and $q$. This angle is vertical to the angle labeled $x^\\circ$, hence, they are congruent. So, the measure of this first angle is $40^\\circ$.\n- A second angle is formed at the intersection of lines $p$ and $m$. This angle lies adjacent to the angle labeled $y^\\circ$, forming a linear pair. Since linear pairs are supplementary, the measure of this second angle is\n$180^\\circ - y^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ$.\n- The third angle is formed at the intersection of lines $q$ and $m$. Since in any triangle the sum of the measures of its interior angles is $180^\\circ$, the measure of the third angle is\n$180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 80^\\circ$.\n\nWe can summarize these angles by adding them to the diagram, shown below.\n\n![](images/4c/4cdbc6e543180e1ebc5220e5d0db4fef545c290bc84f59068bff820379c3530c.png)\n\nNow observe the interior angle at the point where line $q$ intersects line $n$. Since $m \\parallel n$, and the line $q$ acts as a transversal, this angle corresponds to the $80^\\circ$ angle at the intersection of $q$ and $m$. Since corresponding angles are congruent, the measure of this angle is also $80^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/64/64a6f180ea5cc365b8a0ed6e3cb15c34832da15299e648c07efa56c68ce980b6.png)\n\nAt the intersection of lines $q$ and $n$, the two angles labeled $z^\\circ$ form a straight angle on $q$ with the $80^\\circ$ angle we just found. Since a straight angle measures $180^\\circ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{∘} + z^{∘} + 80^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 2z^{∘} = \\mid 100^{∘} \\\\\nz^{∘} &= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $z = 50$."] [17592186221072 :question/worked-solution "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & > 105 \\\\ b + a & > b + 105 \\\\ 180 & > b + 105 \\\\ 75 & > b \\\\ b & < 75\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186221088 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( 2 x - 3 ) - 4 ( y + 4 ) = 38 \\\\ ( 2 x - 3 ) + 4 ( y + 4 ) = 82\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5(2x - 3)$?"] [17592186221089 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [17592186221099 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 3 y = 9 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t,\\, 2t-3)$\n2. $(3t-9,\\, t)$\n3. $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$"] [17592186221099 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2t - 3$\nHence, $(t,\\, 2t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $x$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; 6x|= 3y + 9; x|= \\frac{3y + 9}{6}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}y + \\frac{3}{2}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}t + \\frac{3}{2}$\nThis gives $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t + \\dfrac{3}{2},\\, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-9,\\, t)$. Hence, $(3t-9,\\, t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(4t,h(t))$. So, we set $x=4t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y=9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2(4t) - 3; y|= 8t - 3$\nwhich holds for all $t$. Hence, $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I and III.**"] [17592186221129 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $12$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$S|= 2 \\cdot (base area) + (lateral surface area); = 2 \\cdot πr^{2} + 2πrh$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186221136 :question/problem "Triangles $PQR$ and $XYZ$ are such that\n$PQ=5,\\quad QR = 7, \\quad\\text{and}\\quad XY=15,\\quad YZ=21$.\nWhich of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. The lengths of the sides $\\overline{PR}$ and $\\overline{XZ}$.\n2. $m\\angle{P} = m\\angle{X}$.\n3. The length of the side $\\overline{XZ}$.\n4. No additional information is needed."] [17592186221140 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nIn the case of $\\triangle{XYZ}$, from the diagram we can see that its base is\n\n$$\nb = 3 + 17 = 20\n$$\n\nand its corresponding height is\n$h = q$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{XYZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot q}{2} \\\\\n&= 10q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the area is between $70$ and $90$, inclusive. Thus, we have the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n70 \\leq 10q \\leq 90\n$$\n\nDividing by $10$ to isolate $q$, we obtain\n\n$$\n7 \\leq q \\leq 9\n$$\n\nSince $q$ is an integer, its possible values are $7$, $8$, and $9$.\n\nLooking at the diagram again, we can see that the two right triangles are similar: each has a right angle, and they share the angle $\\angle{X}$. As a consequence of this similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{q} = \\dfrac{3}{3 + 17}\n$$\n\nSolving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{3 + 17} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{20} \\\\\np &= \\frac{3q}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the possible values of $q$, we will find the possible values of $p$.\n\n- For $q = 7$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(7)}{20} = \\dfrac{21}{20}$.\n- For $q = 8$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(8)}{20} = \\dfrac{24}{20} = \\dfrac{6}{5}$.\n- For $q = 9$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(9)}{20} = \\dfrac{27}{20}$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $p$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{27}{20}}\n$$"] [17592186221154 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).$"] [17592186221155 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$"] [17592186221159 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$"] [17592186221164 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1.$\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |"] [17592186221168 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for $s > a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a,$ and we have\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a.$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}.$\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}.$"] [17592186221176 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32$\nand\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.$"] [17592186221201 :question/problem "$$\nF(x) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}- 2 x , & x \\in  [ - 2 , 0 ) \\\\ {x}^{2} , & x \\in  [ 0 , 2 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\nI. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nII. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nIII. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186221201 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)"] [17592186221583 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x + 9 y = 40 \\\\ 3 x + 2 y = 15\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186221584 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}18 x + y = 30 \\\\ 9 x + y = 48\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221585 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221612 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [17592186221613 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 10 p + 6 q = 144 \\\\ 6 p - 10 q = - 96\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [17592186221617 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 5 x - 4 y = - 26\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $5x - y$?"] [17592186221618 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $3x + 2y$?"] [17592186221621 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{10}{3}} x - 4 y = 50 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}} x - 4 y = 26\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{20}{3}x - 12y$?"] [17592186221622 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{3}{5}x - y$?"] [17592186221624 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 x + 4 \\\\ y = a x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $a$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?"] [17592186221625 :question/problem "The equations\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 5 y = 8 \\\\ y + 3 k y = 2 x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nform a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186221629 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = 8 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = - 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186221630 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186221637 :question/problem "The equations\n$\\frac{5}{3}y - \\frac{1}{5}x = \\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$-\\frac{5}{3}y + \\frac{2}{3}x + 1 = py + \\frac{7}{3}$,\nwhere $p$ is a constant, form a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221638 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221642 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 10 \\\\ 2 ( x + y ) + 5 y = 40\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [17592186221643 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = 12 \\\\ 4 ( x + 3 y ) + y = 55\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [17592186221692 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 6 \\\\ 3 x + y = 3\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWhich of the following graphs represents the system of linear equations above?"] [17592186221734 :question/problem "The functions $f$ and $g$ are defined as\n$f(x) = \\frac{3}{4}x - 5andg(x) = \\frac{1}{2}x + 9$.\nIf the function $h$ is defined as\n$h(x) = f(x) + g(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = h(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?The $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $h(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186221735 :question/problem "The functions $u$ and $v$ are defined as\n$u(x) = \\frac{4}{5}x - 8andv(x) = \\frac{2}{5}x + 6$.\nIf the function $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?The $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186221829 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $s$ is a constant.\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 + 7 m = s w \\\\ 7 m + 2 w = - 4 w + 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $s$?$s =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221830 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right.$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?$a =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186221846 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?"] [17592186221856 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?"] [17592186221862 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$"] [17592186221863 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$"] [17592186221910 :question/problem "Triangles $ABC$ and $DEF$ are such that\n$AB = 9, AC = 10, andDE = 18, DF = 20$.\nWhich of the following additional pieces of information is sufficient to determine whether these triangles are similar?\n\n1. $m∠C = m∠F$.\n2. The lengths of the sides $BC^(―)$ and $EF^(―)$.\n3. $m∠B = m∠E$.\n4. No additional information is needed."] [17592186221929 :question/problem "Given that $L{e^{at}} = \\frac{1}{s - a}$ for $s > a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{2τ}dτ$\nfor $s > 2$."] [17592186221931 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$"] [17592186221936 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ}$ given $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A^{′}(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) = 0$. Then,\n\n$f^{′}(τ)$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186221938 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$"] [17592186221943 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $L{u(t - 4)\\sin (10πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [17592186221944 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221948 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{′}(s) = \\frac{d}{ds}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{′}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t)) =-te^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{′}(s) =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}te^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe integral in line $L1$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}."] [17592186221949 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = y(x^{2} - 2), y(-1) =-1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = 1$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{19}{24}$ |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{24}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1) \\approx$ {{field-4}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186221951 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/80/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)"] [17592186221952 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)"] [17592186221953 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186221954 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?"] [17592186221956 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{″} - 7xy^{′} + xy = x, y(-1) = 3, y^{′}(-1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(0) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}."] [17592186221957 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186221961 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?"] [17592186221965 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [0, 0]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186221966 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - x ( t ) + 2 y ( t ) - 4 \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t ) - 4 y ( t ) + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 2]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186221967 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} =-5x + y, y(-1) =-1$\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx = \\frac{1}{3}$. Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_{new}$, in terms of the current values $x$ and $y$, and the new value $x_{new}$."] [17592186221968 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} =-4x + 2xy, y(3) = 5$\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_{new}$, in terms of the new value $x_{new}$ and the current value $y$."] [17592186221969 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {y}^{2} ( t ) + \\cos   ( \\pi  x ( t ) ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 1]^{T}$ is given as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$ {{field-1}}\n\nAt this point, the system is\n{{field-2}}\nat $x^{∗}$."] [17592186221971 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [17592186221973 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 4y = 3x, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z = y^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}."] [17592186221976 :question/problem "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2, 1]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186221992 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′} & =3x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3x-2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3x-2y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3x-2yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-1)+5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈0$\nand ${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈4.$"] [17592186221993 :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{t} \\\\ -6e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ -2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ R$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ above. Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${x_{1}(t), x_{2}(t)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$."] [17592186221997 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bc608bdc2cfd22f6fad528c0b2d3cd421355e4bef810a862b4585a2f85409a3.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png)"] [17592186221998 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 3y = 0, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and $y^{′}(3)$.$-9$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186222004 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}."] [17592186222005 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [17592186222007 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186222008 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2x+1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+1 & 2y \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0)+1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalue of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1,\\text{ }3.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$"] [17592186222011 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈2.67.$"] [17592186222013 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = y(x^{4} + 1), y(0) = 3$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$y\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186222040 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} + π^{2}y = f(x)$, where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼1 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\sin (nπx)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function."] [17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$"] [17592186222519 :tutorial/content "When a Fourier series represents a function $f$, it is often much easier to differentiate the series term-by-term than to recompute a brand-new Fourier series from scratch. \n\nHowever, term-by-term differentiation is only guaranteed to work when the underlying periodic function is sufficiently \"well-behaved\".\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term differentiation of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is\n\n- piecewise smooth, and\n\n- continuous.\n\nThen, the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} -\\dfrac{n\\pi a_n}{L}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+\\dfrac{n\\pi b_n}{L}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $f(x)$ is the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ defined on $x \\in [-L,L),$ then the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x)$ provided that\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-L,L),$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-L,L),$ and\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -L^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to L^-} F(x).$\n\n**Note:** The constant term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$ disappears after differentiating, since $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this theorem to a specific example to see how it works in practice."] [17592186222529 :tutorial/content "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!"] [17592186224846 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ 7m + 2m}_{9m} + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ m + 4m}_{5m} + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224867 :question/problem "Consider the functions\n$f(x) = x^{2} + b x, \\qquad g(x) = 2 x^{2} - 4 x$\nwhere $b$ is a constant. If\n$f(x) \\cdot g(x) = 2 x^{4} + 8 x^{3} - 24 x^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $b$?"] [17592186224872 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{b - 2ub}{2u^2 - 2ub} - \\dfrac{1 + b}{u - b}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{-2ub - 1}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub - 2u^2}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - ub - 2u^2}{2u^2 - 2ub}$\n4. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u^2 - 2ub}$"] [17592186224880 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\dfrac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\dfrac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. What are the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186224880 :question/worked-solution "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 ) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 0; r^{2}|= k^{2}; r|= ± k$.\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 4; r^{2} - k^{2}|= 16; r^{2}|= k^{2} + 16; r|= ± \\sqrt{k^{2} + 16}$.\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{16}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{4}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4; \\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4.✓$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution."] [17592186224881 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first combine like terms and square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 - \\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 2\\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3x} + 1)^{2} &= (2\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} \\\\\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting equation still contains a radical term. To solve, we isolate this term and square both sides again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x - 9 \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x - 9)^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} - 18x + 81 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 30x + 81 \\\\\nx^{2} - 30x + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 27)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=27$ and $x=3$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=27$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 27} + 1 - \\sqrt{27 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{27 - 2}; \\sqrt{81} + 1 - \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{25}; 9 + 1 - 5|= ^(?)5; 5|= 5.✓$\nHence, $x=27$ is a valid solution.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 3} + 1 - \\sqrt{3 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 2}; \\sqrt{9} + 1 - \\sqrt{1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{1}; 3 + 1 - 1|= ^(?)1; 3 \\mid \\ne 1.\\cdot$\nHence, $x=3$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nis $x=27$ only."] [17592186224882 :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the given equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4q + 28})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n4q + 28 &= (q - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, moving all terms to one side, and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q + 28 &= q^{2} - 2q + 1 \\\\\n0 &= q^{2} - 6q - 27 \\\\\n0 &= (q + 3)(q - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two possible solutions: $q=-3$ or $q=9$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions, by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $q=-3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(-3) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{((-3) - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{-12 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16}.✓$\nHence, $q=-3$ *is* a valid solution.\n- For $q=9$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(9) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(9 - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{36 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{8^{2}}; \\sqrt{64}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{64}.✓$\nHence, $q=9$ *is* a valid solution.\n\nSo, the valid solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=9$.\nTherefore, the smallest real solution is $q=-3$."] [17592186224883 :question/problem "What value of $n$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$\\dfrac{n^{2}}{n^{2} + 5n + 6} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}$"] [17592186224889 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w{:}$\n$\\dfrac{24}{x} = \\dfrac{24}{y} + \\dfrac{24}{z} - \\dfrac{24}{w}$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $x$, $z$, and $w$."] [17592186224897 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y - m = 4 x - 2 \\\\ y + m = {x}^{2} + 2 x\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?"] [17592186224898 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 3 \\\\ x - y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?"] [17592186224898 :question/worked-solution "We are given a system of equations and asked to find $y_1 + y_2$, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of the two points where the graphs intersect.\n\nFirst, we substitute the expression for $y$ into the second equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 2 \\\\\nx - (x^{2} - 5x + 3) &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 3 &= 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation by factoring. We begin by finding two numbers that multiply to $5$ and add to $-6$. These are $-1$ and $-5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} \\underbrace{-5x - x}_{-6x} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) - 1(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 5)(x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x^2 - 5x + 3{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x_1 = 5$, then\n$y_1 = 5^2 - 5(5) + 3 = 25 - 25 + 3 = 3$\n- If $x_2 = 1$, then\n$y_2 = 1^2 - 5(1)+3=1 - 5 + 3 = -1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = 3 + (-1) = 2\n$$"] [17592186224899 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x - y = 10 \\\\ x + y = {x}^{2} - 10\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nWhich ordered pair is a solution to the system of equations above?"] [17592186224899 :question/worked-solution "To solve the system, we first use the linear equation to express one variable in terms of the other. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\ny = x - 10\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into the second equation and solve:\n\nHence, applying the zero product rule yields\n\n$$\nx = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y$ using\n\n$$\ny = x - 10{:}\n$$\n\n- If $x = 0$, then\n$y = 0 - 10 = -10$.\n- If $x = 2$, then\n$y = 2 - 10 = -8$.\n\nSo the two solutions to the system are $(0, -10)$ and $(2, -8)$.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, we have solution $(2, -8)$."] [17592186224900 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{u}^{2} = - 11 u + v \\\\ v = 11 u + 121\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $uv$?"] [17592186224900 :question/worked-solution "We are given a system of equations involving a quadratic and a linear equation. To solve, we substitute the second equation into the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= -11u + v \\\\\nu^{2} &= -11u + (11u + 121) \\\\\nu^{2} &= 121\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides to solve for $u{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{u^{2}} &= \\sqrt{121} \\\\\n\\mid u \\mid &= 11 \\\\\nu &= ± 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute each $u$-value into the second equation to find the corresponding $v$-values:\n\n- If $u = 11$, then\n$v|= 11u + 121; = 11(11) + 121; = 242$.\n- If $u = -11$, then\n$v|= 11(-11) + 121; =-121 + 121; = 0$.\n\nFinally, we compute $uv$ in each case:\n\n- If $u = 11,\\ v = 242$, then\n$uv = 11 \\cdot 242 = 2662$.\n- If $u = -11,\\ v = 0$, then\n$uv = -11 \\cdot 0 = 0$.\n\nAmong the given answer choices, the only possible value of $uv$ is $0$."] [17592186224901 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = ( x + 3 ) ( x - 6 ) \\\\ y = 9 x - 54\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nWhat is the ordered pair $(x, y)$ that satisfies the given system of equations?"] [17592186224947 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $q$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $q$ must have the form\n\n$$\nq(x) = a \\cdot k^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $k$ is the growth factor.\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$ and $k$.\n\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$q(0)|= b^{-7}; a \\cdot k^{0}|= b^{-7}; a|= b^{-7}$.\n- From $x = 2$, we have\n$q(2)|= b^{3}; a \\cdot k^{2}|= b^{3}$.\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= b^{-7} \\\\\na \\cdot k^{2} &= b^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a \\cdot k^{2}}{a} &= \\frac{b^{3}}{b^{-7}} \\\\\nk^{2} &= b^{10} \\\\\nk &= b^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $a=b^{-7}$ and $k=b^{5}$ into the model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(x) &= a \\cdot k^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot (b^{5})^{x} \\\\\n&= b^{-7} \\cdot b^{5x} \\\\\n&= b^{5x - 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that\n\n$$\nq(x) = b^{5x - 7}\n$$\n\nworks for $x = 3{:}$\n\n$$\nq(3) = b^{5(3)-7}=b^{8}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the value in the table.\n\nWe are asked to find $m$ such that\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{23}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nq(m) = b^{5m - 7}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nb^{5m - 7} = b^{23}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate the exponents and solve for $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5m - 7 &= 23 \\\\\n5m &= 30 \\\\\nm &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224948 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $g$ is a quadratic function, so the relationship between $x$ and $g$ must have the form\n\n$$\ng(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nWe will use the values from the table to write a system of equations for the parameters $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n\n- From $x = -2$, we have\n$g(-2)|= 0; a(-2)^{2} + b(-2) + c|= 0; 4a - 2b + c|= 0$.\n- From $x = 1$, we have\n$g(1)|=-3; a(1)^{2} + b(1) + c|=-3; a + b + c|=-3$.\n- From $x = 0$, we have\n$g(0)|= 2; a(0)^{2} + b(0) + c|= 2; c|= 2$.\n\nNow, we substitute $c = 2$ into the other two equations.\n\n- From $4a - 2b + c = 0$, we have\n$4a - 2b + 2|= 0; 4a - 2b|=-2; 2a - b|=-1$.\n- From $a + b + c = -3$, we have\n$a + b + 2|=-3; a + b|=-5$.\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - b &= -1 \\\\\na + b &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= -6 \\\\\na &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the value $a=-2$ into\n\n$$\na + b = -5{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n-2 + b = -5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b = -3\n$$\n\nFinally, collecting $a=-2$, $b=-3$, and $c=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= ax^{2} + bx + c \\\\\n&=-2x^{2} - 3x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186224950 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $h$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $h$ must have the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at $x = 0$ is $1$, so\n\n$$\nh(0) = a = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the base $b$, we use the value at $x = 1{:}$\n\n$$\nh(1) = b = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this works for other values in the table.\n\n- When $x = 2$, we have\n$h(2)= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{49}. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- When $x = -1$, we have\n$h(-1) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^{-1} = 7. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nBoth values match the table. So, the equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$"] [17592186224982 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of the circle, we draw line segments from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/aecff45af3948fe8518a830c2190cca88d58c6b9457c1860f618bb34fa3ae00c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{ML}{2} = \\dfrac{72}{2} = 36$\n- The shorter leg is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n36 &= r \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $12\\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$."] [17592186224983 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5$.\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$."] [17592186224987 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$"] [17592186224995 :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."] [17592186224999 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186225011 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x - x^{2}})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{-3x - 5})^{2} \\\\\nx - x^{2} &= -3x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms to one side and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 5 &= x - x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=-1$ and $x=5$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$\\sqrt{-1 - (-1)^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(-1) - 5}; \\sqrt{-1 - 1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 5}; \\sqrt{-2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-2}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$\\sqrt{5 - 5^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(5) - 5}; \\sqrt{5 - 25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-15 - 5}; \\sqrt{-20}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-20}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=5$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."] [17592186225014 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186225016 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\n{y = 13; y = \\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $y = 13$.\n\nSubstituting $y = 13$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\mid -3x + 4 \\mid = 13\n$$\n\nThis absolute value equation has two cases:\n\n- If $-3x + 4 > 0$, we have\n$-3x + 4|= 13; - 3x|= 9; x|=-3$.\n- If $-3x + 4 < 0$, we have\n$-3x + 4|=-13; - 3x|=-17; x|= \\frac{17}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, $x = -3$ and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{17}{3}\n$$\n\nare the two solutions of the given system of equations."] [17592186225018 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 144 \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225019 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?"] [17592186225020 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection."] [17592186225024 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, with its maximum at $x=0$, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, and has no maximum.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$,\nthe base is $1.2>1$. So, the function has no maximum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n- For the function $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$g(0)|= 27 \\cdot (0.75)^{0}; = 27$.\nThus, the maximum value is $27$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the maximum as a coefficient.\n- For the function $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$h(0)|= 9 \\cdot (0.75)^{0} + 4; = 9 + 4; = 13$.\nThus, the maximum value is $13$. However, neither the constant term ($4$) nor the coefficient ($9$) equals $13$. Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186225027 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its **minimum** at $x=1$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$,\nthe base is $0.7\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 1$ and has **no** minimum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function\n$g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$,\nthe base is $1.4>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$g(1)|= 12 \\cdot (1.4)^{1} - 3; = 12 \\cdot 1.4 - 3; = 16.8 - 3; = 13.8$.\nThe minimum value is $13.8$, which is **not** equal to the coefficient ($12$) or the constant term $(-3)$. Hence, the equation of $g$ does *not* display the minimum.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$,\nthe base is $1.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$h(1)|= 15 \\cdot (1.5)^{1} - 7.5; = 15 \\cdot 1.5 - 7.5; = 22.5 - 7.5; = 15$.\nThus, the minimum value is $15$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $h$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186225031 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [17592186225033 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we have $g(0) = -1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $b{:}$\n$g(0)|=-1; a^{0} + b|=-1; 1 + b|=-1; b|=-2$\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(3,25)$, we have $g(3) = 25$. Then, using the fact that $b=-2$, we can determine the value of $c$, as follows:\n$g(3)|= 25; a^{3} + (-2)|= 25; a^{3} - 2|= 25; a^{3}|= 27; a|= \\sqrt[3]{27}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 + (-2) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186225041 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186225043 :question/worked-solution "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4, -8)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2} - 8x + 16) - 8 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} - 8ax + 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= -8a \\\\\nc &= 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + (-8a) + (16a - 8) \\\\\n&= 9a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at two points and has its vertex at $(4, -8)$ below the $x$-axis, it must open upward, meaning $a > 0$.\n\nNow, let's go through all the options and check which ones give $a > 0{:}$\n\n- Option I, $-8{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-8; 9a|=-8 + 8; 9a|= 0; a|= 0≯0\\cdot$\n- Option II, $-1{:}$\n$9a - 8|=-1; 9a|=-1 + 8; 9a|= 7; a|= \\frac{7}{9} > 0✓$\n- Option III, $10{:}$\n$9a - 8|= 10; 9a|= 10 + 8; 9a|= 18; a|= 2 > 0✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186225046 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186225058 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?"] [17592186225058 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [17592186225060 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?"] [17592186225063 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225064 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = - 6 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 15\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?"] [17592186225065 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 8 y = 32 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = - 32\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225071 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|= \\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); =-\\frac{10}{3}; < 0$.\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); = \\frac{10}{3}; ≮0$.\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$"] [17592186225072 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the discriminant of the factor $x^2+x+2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal D = 1^2-4(1)(2) = -7 < 0\n$$\n\nso our cubic cannot be factored any further over the real numbers.\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has an $x$-intercept at $x=3$.\n- $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= (0 - 3)(0^{2} + 0 + 2); = (-3) \\cdot (2); =-6$.\n\nThe corresponding partial graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6efc85f87a2ca97602de742653ccfe08b08cd409a35bca97b5dd3470984f71f0.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a \\leq 3\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph.\nNotice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186225073 :question/problem "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$c(m) = 8 + \\left|2m + m^2\\right|, \\qquad d(z) = \\left|\\dfrac{z - 5}{2z}\\right| + z + 2$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?"] [17592186225074 :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$25 - 5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$-25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0$,\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"] [17592186225428 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$\\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [17592186225430 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}$\n\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?"] [17592186225440 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}$?"] [17592186225441 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\frac{b - 2ub}{2u^{2} - 2ub} - \\frac{1 + b}{u - b}$?"] [17592186225452 :question/problem "Consider the following functions\n$p(x) = x^{2} - 4, q(x) = x - 2$.\n\nWhich of the following expressions is equivalent to $(p(x))/(q(x))$, where $x > 2$?"] [17592186225526 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 64 \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225534 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 13 \\\\ x y = 40\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf one solution to the system of equations above is $(x, y)$, what is the smallest possible value of $x$?\n\n$x =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225535 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} = 5 x + y \\\\ y = - 5 x + 25\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $xy$?"] [17592186225536 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 2 \\\\ x - y = {x}^{2} - 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWhich ordered pair is a solution to the system of equations above?"] [17592186225537 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y + m = 2 x + 9 \\\\ y - m = {x}^{2} - 4 x\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?"] [17592186225553 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 144 \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225566 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 6 \\\\ y = {2}^{x} + x\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225567 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 9 \\\\ y = 4 \\cdot  {2}^{x - 2} + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225568 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 10 \\\\ y = | 3 x + 2 |\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?"] [17592186225569 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 13 \\\\ y = | - 3 x + 4 |\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?"] [17592186225572 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = ( x - 1 ) ( x - 6 ) \\\\ y = 5 x - 30\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWhich ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations?"] [17592186225574 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{u}^{2} = - 11 u + v \\\\ v = 11 u + 121\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $uv$?"] [17592186225575 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 x \\\\ y = {x}^{2} - 18\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$, where $x > 0$. What is the value of $x$?\n\n$x =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225576 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y - m = 4 x - 2 \\\\ y + m = {x}^{2} + 2 x\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?"] [17592186225580 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} + 2 x + 4 \\\\ x + y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?"] [17592186225581 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 3 \\\\ x - y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?"] [17592186225586 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = ( x + 3 ) ( x - 6 ) \\\\ y = 9 x - 54\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nWhich ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations?"] [17592186225591 :question/problem "The functions $s$ and $t$ are defined by the given equations.\n$s(u) = 5 +\\begin{vmatrix}u^{2} - u &, t(p) = & \\frac{2p}{p + 4}\\end{vmatrix}+ p - 1$\nIf $s(1) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $t(c)$?$t(c) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225592 :question/problem "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$c(m) = 8 +\\begin{vmatrix}2m + m^{2} &, d(z) = & \\frac{z - 5}{2z}\\end{vmatrix}+ z + 2$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?$d(c) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225600 :question/problem "Consider the functions\n$f(x) = x^{2} + ax, g(x) = 3x^{2} - 9x$\nwhere $a$ is a constant. If\n$f(x) \\cdot g(x) = 3x^{4} - 15x^{3} + 18x^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $a$?"] [17592186225605 :question/problem "Consider the functions\n$f(x) = x^{2} + bx, g(x) = 2x^{2} - 4x$\nwhere $b$ is a constant. If\n$f(x) \\cdot g(x) = 2x^{4} + 8x^{3} - 24x^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $b$?"] [17592186225703 :question/problem "What value of $x$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$\\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2} - 1} - \\frac{x}{x - 1} = \\frac{4}{x + 1}$"] [17592186225704 :question/problem "What value of $n$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$\\frac{n^{2}}{n^{2} + 5n + 6} - \\frac{2n}{n + 2} =-\\frac{9}{n + 3}$"] [17592186225705 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} + 6$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186225706 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186225827 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 20 x + 13 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225828 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {x}^{2} - 6 x + 10 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186225829 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection.\n\nThe point of intersection is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [17592186225890 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 5 {x}^{2} - 15 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186225894 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 12 x + 20 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186225897 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 8 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186225898 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 4 = \\sqrt{2} \\\\ ( 2 x - 4 {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225899 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225900 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 = ( x + 4 y ) ( x - 3 y ) \\\\ 7 x - 21 y = 35\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186225901 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 12 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 42\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x + y$?"] [17592186225902 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} - {y}^{2} = 72 \\\\ 2 x + 2 y = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x - y$?"] [17592186225903 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 4 y = 36 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186228736 :question/worked-solution "The average (or mean) is calculated by dividing the sum of the data points by the number of data points.\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Consider statement I. We are given the total sales amount $(\\[math]1,000)$ and the number of salespeople $(5)$. So, we can compute the average, given by\n$\\text{Average} = \\dfrac{1,000}{5} = \\[/math]200$.\nTherefore, the average sales per person *can* be determined.\n- Consider statement II. We know one salesperson made $\\[math]400$, but we do not know the sales amounts of the others. It is possible that another salesperson made more. Therefore, the greatest sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n- Consider statement III. Similarly, we cannot determine the least sales without knowing the sales for each person. Therefore, the least sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186228739 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the mean and median of the five numbers are not equal.\n\nLet’s begin by writing an expression for the mean. The mean of the five values is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11 + w + 14 + 16 + 20}{5} = \\dfrac{61+w}{5}\n$$\n\nAlso, since there are five numbers, the median will be the $3$rd value when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, let’s test each value of $w$ in turn.\n\n- If $w = 13$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 13,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $14$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 13}{5} = \\frac{74}{5} = 14.8$.\nSince the mean and median are not equal, $13$ *is* a possible value of $w$.\n- If $w = 14$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 14,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $14$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 14}{5} = \\frac{75}{5} = 15$.\nSince the mean and median are not equal, $14$ *is* a possible value of $w$.\n- If $w = 19$, then the list arranged in order from smallest to greatest is\n$11,\\: 14,\\: 16,\\: 19,\\: 20$.\nIn this case, the median is $16$, and the mean is\n$\\frac{61 + 19}{5} = \\frac{80}{5} = 16$.\nSince the mean and median are equal, $19$ *is not* a possible value of $w$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186228744 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the mean of the $9$ known values is $48$. Using this, we can compute the sum $S$ of those $9$ values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean &= \\frac{S}{9} \\\\\nS &= 9 \\cdot Mean \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 48 \\\\\n&= 432\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery value in dataset $Q$ is a positive integer less than $70$, and the mean of dataset $Q$ is an integer that is greater than $48$. Let's consider possible values for the mean, starting with the smallest.\n\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $49$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 49 = 490$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$490 - S = 490 - 432 = 58$.\nSince $58 < 70$, this value *can* belong to the dataset.\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $50$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 50 = 500$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$500 - S = 500 - 432 = 68$.\nSince $68 < 70$, this value *also* can belong to the dataset.\n- If the mean of $Q$ is $51$, then the total sum of the dataset is\n$10 \\cdot 51 = 510$.\nSubtracting the sum of the nine given integers, the missing value is\n$510 - S = 510 - 432 = 78$.\nSince $78 > 70$, this value *cannot* belong to the dataset.\n\nWe stop here because increasing the mean any further would only increase the missing value, which would continue to violate the constraint that all values must be less than $70$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible missing value, and hence, the largest value in dataset $Q$, is ${68}$."] [17592186228748 :question/worked-solution "We proceed to calculate the mean, median, and mode separately.\n\n- The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.Therefore, for our data set, the mean is\n$mean|= (7(2) + 8(3) + 9(6) + 10(5) + 11(3) + 15(1))/(20); = \\frac{14 + 24 + 54 + 50 + 33 + 15}{20}; = \\frac{190}{20}; = 9.5$.\n- The median is the middle value of a data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.Since there are $20$ rent amounts ordered, the median is the average of the two middle values, the $10$th and $11$th, which for these data are both $9$. Then, the median is\n$median|= \\frac{9 + 9}{2}; = \\frac{18}{2}; = 9$.\n- The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.For the data shown, we have\n$\\textrm{mode} = 9$.\n\nTherefore, the correct order is\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = \\text{median} < \\text{mean}\n$$"] [17592186228752 :question/worked-solution "The mean (or average) is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nWe proceed to determine the mean of the data sets from each of the given frequency tables.\n\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$1$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(1) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{61}{8}; \\approx 7.63$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$2$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(2))/(8); = \\frac{52}{8}; = 6.5$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$3$$5$$2$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(3) + 5(2) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{43}{8}; \\approx 5.38$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$3$$8$$2$$11$$1$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(3) + 8(2) + 11(1))/(8); = \\frac{46}{8}; = 5.75$.\n- For the table\nValueFrequency$2$$2$$5$$2$$8$$1$$11$$3$\nthe mean of the data set is\n$mean|= (2(2) + 5(2) + 8(1) + 11(3))/(8); = \\frac{55}{8}; \\approx 6.88$.\n\nTherefore, the frequency table whose data set has the greatest mean is as follows:\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $2$ |\n| $11$ | $3$ |"] [17592186228756 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{-2}{q + 1} + \\dfrac{5}{q - 4}\\right)}$"] [17592186228757 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the expression?\n\n$\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{r + 1} + r - 4\\right)}$"] [17592186228760 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to\n$\\dfrac{4s + 8}{r - 4} + \\dfrac{s(r - 4)}{r^2 s - 4 r s}$,\nwhere $r > 4$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{4rs + 9r + 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n2. $\\dfrac{4rs + 9r - 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n3. $\\dfrac{4s + 8 + r - 4}{r(r - 4)}$\n4. $\\dfrac{4s(r - 4) + 1}{r(r - 4)}$"] [17592186228772 :question/worked-solution "The mean (or average) in a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nSince the table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set, we find the total sum by multiplying each data value by its frequency and then adding all these products together.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean of each data set:\n\n- The mean of the data set $A$ is\n$Mean(A)|= (12(5) + 16(6) + 20(7) + 24(5) + 28(4))/(27); = \\frac{528}{27}; \\approx 19.56$,\nrounded to two decimal places.\n- The mean of the data set $B$ is\n$Mean(B)|= (12(3) + 16(5) + 20(6) + 24(7) + 28(6))/(27); = \\frac{572}{27}; \\approx 21.19$,\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(A) < \\textrm{Mean}(B)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186228774 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set associated with each museum.\n\n- North Museum data points are not ordered, so we order them first from smallest to largest:\n$480,\\: 500,\\: 520, \\: 540, \\: 560$\nThis data set is symmetric and contains $5$ elements, which is an odd number, so the mean and median are both equal to the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(N) = \\textrm{Median}(N) = 520$\n- South Museum data points are already ordered from smallest to largest:\n$300, \\: 480, \\: 500, \\: 510, \\: 520$\nThis data set is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(S)|= \\frac{300 + 480 + 500 + 510 + 520}{5}; = \\frac{2310}{5}; = 462$.\nSince it contains $5$ elements, its median is the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(S) = 500$\n\nSince North Museum has a higher mean and median than South Museum, both statements are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II\"."] [17592186228775 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $P$ is symmetric with $4$ data values in ascending order; it follows that the mean is between the second and third data values, that is, its mean is $12$.\n- Data set $Q$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(Q)|= \\frac{8 + 12 + 12 + 16 + 32}{5}; = \\frac{80}{5}; = 16$.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(P) < \\textrm{Mean}(Q)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [17592186228776 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $G$ is symmetric with $5$ values (an odd number), so its mean is the $3$rd data value in the list:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(G) = 58$\n- Data set $H$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(H)|= \\frac{40 + 57 + 60 + 63 + y}{5}; = \\frac{220 + y}{5}$.\n\nSince the mean of the data set $G$ is the same as the mean of the data set $H$, we equate them and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(H) &= Mean(G) \\\\\n\\frac{220 + y}{5} &= 58 \\\\\n220 + y &= 58 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n220 + y &= 290 \\\\\ny &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $70$."] [17592186228778 :question/worked-solution "The range of a data set equals the difference between the maximum and the minimum.\n\n- For Model $A$, the maximum is $42$ and the minimum is $22$, so the range is\n$\\text{Range}(A) = 42 - 22 = 20$.\n- For Model $B$, the maximum is $38$ and the minimum is $18$, so the range is\n$\\text{Range}(B) = 38 - 18 = 20$.\n\nSubtracting, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\bigl|\\text{Range}(A) - \\text{Range}(B)\\bigr| = \\mid 20 - 20 \\mid = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the positive difference is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}$."] [17592186228783 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 480 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  744 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the machine is painting at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n744\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$"] [17592186228784 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$\\left(\\frac{60s}{min}\\right)^{2}|= \\frac{60^{2}s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = \\frac{3, 600s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.6 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 , 160}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  12.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [17592186228787 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $18 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$1h|= 60min; = 60 \\cdot 1min; = 60 \\cdot 60s; = 3, 600s$.\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 18 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 18 \\cdot  1 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{18 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n18\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186228793 :question/problem "A toy car moves at a speed of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}$. What is its speed in yards per second?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$"] [17592186228830 :question/worked-solution "At $x = 1$, the value of $7x+3$ is greater than the value of $7^x$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7(1) + 3 & >^(?)7^{1} \\\\ 7 + 3 & >^(?)7 \\\\ 10 & > 7.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x = 3$, the value of $7^x$ is greater than the value of $7x+3$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7^{3} & >^(?)7(3) + 3 \\\\ 343 & >^(?)21 + 3 \\\\ 343 & > 24.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, there is a value $c$ between $1$ and $3$ such that if $0 < x < c$, we have $7x+3 > 7^x$, and if $x > c$, we have $7^x > 7x+3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is \"III only\"."] [17592186228833 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}$, where $x\\neq 5$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{7}{(x-5)^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{(x-5)^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{2}{(x-5)^2}$\n4. $\\dfrac{1}{2(x-5)^2}$"] [17592186228835 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186228836 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$."] [17592186228838 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$."] [17592186228844 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186228849 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$\\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186228852 :question/worked-solution "Let's compare the medians and ranges of data sets $C$ and $D{:}$\n\n- Medians:There are $19$ values, so the median of $C$ is the $10$th value when ordered. Counting frequencies across $9,10,11,12,13$ gives cumulative counts $3,7,12,16,19$, so the $10$th value lies at\n$\\text{Median}(C)=11$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the median of $D$ will still be the $10$th value, but shifted down by $12{:}$\n$\\text{Median}(D)=11-12=-1$.Thus, the median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$.\n- Ranges: The range of data set $C{:}$\n$\\text{Range}(C)=\\max(C)-\\min(C)=13-9=4$.After subtracting $12$ from each value in data set $C$, the range of data set $D{:}$\n$Range(D)|= (max(C) - 12) - (min(C) - 12); = max(C) - 12 - min(C) + 12; = max(C) - min(C); = Range(C)$.Thus, the ranges are equal.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is “The median of data set $D$ is smaller than the median of data set $C$, and the range of data set $D$ is equal to the range of data set $C$.”"] [17592186228900 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta &=-2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$"] [17592186228907 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign."] [17592186228909 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$"] [17592186228915 :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ \n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by  $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$."] [17592186228922 :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$"] [17592186228929 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186228933 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$"] [17592186228945 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = 4.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^4\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$"] [17592186228948 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\ny_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\not\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ then \n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = x^{\\lambda_2} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_2)x^n.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2,$ then\n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\in \\mathbb{N},$ then\n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = b_{-1}y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_2}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 9 = 0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $\\lambda=3$ is a double root. So, the indicial roots are equal $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = 3.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nNow, since the roots are equal, the second solution is given by\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $b_n$ are found by substitution."] [17592186228950 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (5\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda = \\dfrac35,$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5}+n)(5(\\frac{3}{5}+n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5n(n+\\frac{3}{5})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n(5n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with $a_0\\neq0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{1(5\\cdot 1+3)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{6}{8}{a}_{0}=\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{2(5\\cdot 2+3)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{26}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}=\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{3/5}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/5}({a}_{0}+\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{3/5}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8/5}+\\frac{9}{52}{x}^{13/5}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186229277 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{4}{x - 3} + \\frac{1}{x + 2} = (mx + n)/((x - 3)(x + 2))$\nis true for all $x > 3$, where $m$ and $n$ are positive constants. What is the value of $mn$?"] [17592186229282 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{4}{y + 5} + \\frac{3}{y - 2}))$"] [17592186229283 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}))$"] [17592186229533 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $\\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}$?"] [17592186229628 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} - 6y = 8e^{2x}, y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 6y = 8e^{2x}$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{6x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186229630 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [17592186229631 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} - 7y = 14\\sin (7x), y(0) = 1$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 7y = 14\\sin (7x)$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{7x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"]}
